WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.875
Friday, four P M. The client says: we're live! Monday, nine A M. Eighty live invoices sit Pending. None were sent.

00:00:07.875 --> 00:00:14.150
The mapping was fine. The go-live itself was wrong. Going live is a small project of its own.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:19.875
Think of moving house. First, you check the new place has water, power, and a working lock.

00:00:19.875 --> 00:00:25.625
You keep the old key for a few days. And for the first nights, you notice every strange noise.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:31.500
Go-live has the same four parts: a decision, a switch, a plan B, and a watch period.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:38.475
First, the decision, called go or no-go. Named people agree that every item on the checklist is true.

00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:46.975
The checklist has four groups: testing, configuration, people and plan, and a safety net. One false hard gate means no-go.

00:00:46.975 --> 00:00:56.300
Second, the switch. Get the partner's production I D and envelope in writing. Check that every live channel points to a real destination.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:02.625
Set Auto Send for live as agreed, and deploy the tested integration with production connections.

00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:08.125
Then promote every relationship, for every document type. Not just the eight fifty.

00:01:08.125 --> 00:01:17.175
A real lesson: a partner flipped to production, but some of our relationships were still in a go-live stage. Orders kept landing on test.

00:01:17.175 --> 00:01:24.401
So reload and re-verify everything. Then watch the first live documents end to end, in both directions.

00:01:24.401 --> 00:01:30.551
Third, plan B, the rollback plan. Write down the triggers, who decides, and the steps to go back.

00:01:30.551 --> 00:01:36.101
Most go-lives never use it. But writing it forces you to know how the old way works.

00:01:36.101 --> 00:01:43.076
Also plan the cutover date, outside the partner's blackout windows, and never at five P M on a Friday.

00:01:43.076 --> 00:01:50.751
Fourth, hypercare, the watch period. Check constantly at first, then every few hours, then daily for a week or two.

00:01:50.751 --> 00:01:55.926
In Orderful, watch four statuses: Invalid, Pending, Failed, and Overdue.

00:01:55.926 --> 00:02:02.901
Overdue means the partner's nine ninety seven, their message saying I got your file, did not arrive in time.

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:11.476
But Overdue is a symptom, not a cause. Maybe your document never really left. Maybe their nine ninety seven went to the wrong I D.

00:02:11.476 --> 00:02:18.876
Maybe they send them late, or they rejected your file. Treat Overdue as a question to answer, not a status to ignore.

00:02:18.876 --> 00:02:24.326
When things are stable for several days, hand the account to support with a short runbook.

00:02:24.326 --> 00:02:30.176
In one line: decide with a checklist, switch everything, keep a plan B, and watch closely.

00:02:30.176 --> 00:02:35.026
Next, we look at web E D I, and the shipping labels on every carton.

